# Copy to .env and adjust.

# Where your media is on the server (subfolders are scanned too).
# DVD images (.iso) and audio CD images (.cue + .bin) can live in different places:
DVD_DIR=/srv/dvds
CD_DIR=/srv/cds
# Blu-ray images (.iso), unprotected (decrypted when they were made). Optional: a Blu-ray among the DVDs, or in
# MEDIA_DIR, is recognized by its content anyway.
BLURAY_DIR=
# Or keep everything in one folder and set only this (used when DVD_DIR / CD_DIR are not set):
# MEDIA_DIR=/srv/media

# IP of the server on your network (the same address your TV/browser uses to reach it).
# Required for WebRTC.
SERVER_IP=192.168.0.10

# Password the catalog uses to log into the player (local network / Tailscale only)
NEKO_PASSWORD=neko
NEKO_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-this-password

# Ports on the server
CATALOG_PORT=8080
NEKO_PORT=8081

# Optional: public URL of the player if it sits behind a reverse proxy (e.g. https://player.example.com)
NEKO_PUBLIC_URL=

# Stream quality. A DVD is 720x480 (NTSC) or 720x576 (PAL), so 1024x576 (16:9 at DVD height) keeps all its
# detail: a higher resolution only scales it up on the server instead of on the TV. The bit rate is what
# removes blocks and blur in fast scenes. Higher values of all three cost server CPU and network bandwidth.
# The stream is H.264; a TV or browser that shows a black picture with sound needs VP8 (see the README).
STREAM_RESOLUTION=1024x576
# 30 suits NTSC discs, 25 suits PAL discs.
STREAM_FPS=30
# Video bit rate in kbit/s (Neko's own default is about 2000).
STREAM_BITRATE=4000
# Blu-ray: while one plays, the catalog switches the screen to this size (a Blu-ray is 1920x1080; a DVD
# would gain nothing from it) and the video to BLURAY_BITRATE, used whenever the screen is taller than 720
# lines. It costs server CPU. Leave BLURAY_RESOLUTION empty to keep one size for every disc.
BLURAY_RESOLUTION=1920x1080
BLURAY_BITRATE=8000
# Bit rate in kbit/s of the plain video (HLS) for TVs that cannot use WebRTC, such as LG webOS 3 TVs.
# It only runs while such a TV plays a disc. See "The video stream" in docs/API.md.
# HLS_BITRATE=2500
# How VLC removes the combing of interlaced DVDs: yadif (good), yadif2x (smoother, more CPU), blend, bob,
# or off. Discs flagged as progressive are left alone.
DVD_DEINTERLACE=yadif

# DVD language preferences, like the settings of a DVD player: two-letter codes such as pt, es or fr.
# Many discs show their menus in the player's menu language, which is English when this is empty.
# Leave a setting empty to let the disc choose (subtitles then stay as the disc starts them).
DVD_MENU_LANGUAGE=
DVD_AUDIO_LANGUAGE=
DVD_SUBTITLE_LANGUAGE=

# Optional: let another app (for example a TV app) call the API from a different origin.
# Comma-separated origins, or * for any. Leave empty to keep it off. A packaged app that runs from
# a file:// URL sends the origin "null". There is no login, so only enable this on a trusted network.
CORS_ORIGIN=

# Optional: automatic covers, synopses and genres (free key at themoviedb.org)
TMDB_API_KEY=
# Language of TMDB results, e.g. en-US, pt-BR, es-ES
TMDB_LANGUAGE=en-US

# Audio CDs are looked up on MusicBrainz / Cover Art Archive (no key needed).
# MusicBrainz asks for a contact (e-mail or project URL) in the User-Agent.
MUSICBRAINZ_CONTACT=

# Optional: pin the player image to a specific release tag if a newer Neko release changes behavior
# NEKO_IMAGE=ghcr.io/m1k1o/neko/vlc:latest
